# Verifier

The judgement of a submission, start to finish: read it, probe the names it
declares, check the assembled document with the kernel, apply the acceptance
policy. A worker runs this to learn how its attempt went; the trusted verifier
runs exactly the same function on its own pinned toolchain before anything is
recorded as proved, so the two can only disagree if their toolchains do.

The order is cheapest-first, and each step only runs when the steps before it let
the submission through: a submission refused by its reading never costs a kernel
run, and a submission that shadows a name never reaches the kernel at all.

Import Shared.
Import Submission.
Import Verdict.
Import Host.
Import Reference.

## Note
What every declared name's probe found, in order, or why the probes could not
run. The probe of a name checks the preamble followed by `Check <name>.`.

## To probeNames (tc: Toolchain, preamble: Text, names: Seq of Text) -> Result of Seq of Text and Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for name in names:
        Inspect kernelRun(tc, nameProbeDocument(preamble, name)):
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error e.
            When Ok (run):
                If run's timedOut:
                    Push "unknown" to out.
                Otherwise:
                    Push probeOutcome(run's exitCode, run's stdout, name) to out.
    Return a new Ok with value out.

## Note
The verdict on `submission` as a proof of the statement `statementDoc`, whose goal
is `goalName`; the proof must be the theorem `proofNameFor(goalName)`.

## To judgeSubmission (tc: Toolchain, statementDoc: Text, goalName: Text, submission: Text) -> Verdict:
    Let proofName be proofNameFor(goalName).
    Let problems be submissionProblems(submission, proofName, goalName).
    If length of problems is greater than 0:
        Return refusedSubmissionVerdict(problems).
    Let names be submissionNames(submission).
    Inspect probeNames(tc, statementDoc, names):
        When Err (e):
            Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
        When Ok (outcomes):
            Inspect nameProbeVerdict("submission", "the statement or the prelude", names, outcomes):
                When Some (v):
                    Return v.
                When None:
                    Let document be assembleProofDocument(statementDoc, submission, proofName).
                    Inspect kernelRun(tc, document):
                        When Err (e):
                            Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                        When Ok (run):
                            Return kernelVerdict(run's exitCode, run's stdout, run's timedOut, proofName, goalName).
    Return infrastructureVerdict("unjudged").

## Note
The document that checks a statement on its own: the statement and one closing
`Check <goal>.`, whose output must be `<goal> : Prop`.

## To statementCheckDocument (statementDoc: Text, goalName: Text) -> Text:
    Return trimEnd(statementDoc) + "\n\n/-! ## Closing check, appended by the factory -/\n\nCheck " + goalName + ".\n".

## Note
The verdict on a statement document before it can be reviewed: it reads as a
statement, every name it declares is new to the prelude, it checks, and its goal
is a proposition. `verified` here means well-formed, never true; whether it says
what the problem says is the semantic review's question.

## To judgeStatement (tc: Toolchain, statementDoc: Text, goalName: Text) -> Verdict:
    Let problems be statementProblems(statementDoc, goalName).
    If length of problems is greater than 0:
        Return newVerdict("rejected", 0, join(problems, "; "), [], "the statement was refused before the kernel saw it").
    Let mutable names be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for block in (kernelBlocks(statementDoc))'s items:
        Repeat for name in declaredNames(block):
            Push name to names.
    Inspect probeNames(tc, "", names):
        When Err (e):
            Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
        When Ok (outcomes):
            Inspect nameProbeVerdict("statement", "the prelude", names, outcomes):
                When Some (v):
                    Return v.
                When None:
                    Inspect kernelRun(tc, statementCheckDocument(statementDoc, goalName)):
                        When Err (e):
                            Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                        When Ok (run):
                            If run's timedOut:
                                Return infrastructureVerdict("largo ran past its deadline").
                            Inspect parseKernelReport(run's stdout):
                                When Err (e):
                                    Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                                When Ok (report):
                                    If report's checked is equal to false:
                                        Return newVerdict("rejected", report's failedBlock, report's error, [], "the kernel refused the statement").
                                    Let n be length of report's outputs.
                                    If n is equal to 0 or item n of report's outputs is not equal to goalName + " : Prop":
                                        Return newVerdict("rejected", n, "the goal is not a proposition", [], "the statement's goal must be a Prop").
                                    Return newVerdict("verified", 0, "", [], "the statement checks and its goal is a proposition").
    Return infrastructureVerdict("unjudged").

## Note
Everything wrong with the kernel reference, checked against the kernel itself: a
positive example that does not check, a failure example that checks or fails with
another error, a promised prelude name that is not bound. The empty list is the
reference's licence to be put in front of a model.

## To referenceProblems (tc: Toolchain) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for e in allExamples():
        Inspect kernelRun(tc, e's document):
            When Err (why):
                Push "{e's name}: " + why to out.
            When Ok (run):
                Inspect parseKernelReport(run's stdout):
                    When Err (why):
                        Push "{e's name}: " + why to out.
                    When Ok (report):
                        If e's expectError is equal to "":
                            If report's checked is equal to false:
                                Push "{e's name}: does not check: " + report's error to out.
                        Otherwise:
                            If report's checked:
                                Push "{e's name}: checks, but is shown as a refusal" to out.
                            Otherwise:
                                If report's error contains e's expectError is equal to false:
                                    Push "{e's name}: refused with `{report's error}`, not `{e's expectError}`" to out.
    Let mutable checks be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for name in referenceNames():
        Push "Check " + name + "." to checks.
    Inspect kernelRun(tc, join(checks, "\n\n") + "\n"):
        When Err (why):
            Push "prelude names: " + why to out.
        When Ok (run):
            Inspect parseKernelReport(run's stdout):
                When Err (why):
                    Push "prelude names: " + why to out.
                When Ok (report):
                    If report's checked is equal to false:
                        Push "prelude names: block {report's failedBlock}: " + report's error to out.
    Return out.

## Test: a statement check document ends with one Check of the goal

Expect that statementCheckDocument("Definition goal : Prop := True.\n", "goal") is equal to "Definition goal : Prop := True.\n\n/-! ## Closing check, appended by the factory -/\n\nCheck goal.\n".

## Test: a refused submission never reaches the kernel

Let v be judgeSubmission(defaultToolchain(), fixtureStatement(), "goal", "Notation \"x\" := Zero.\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.").
Expect that v's kind is equal to "rejected".
Expect that v's error is equal to "block 1 uses `Notation`, which a submission may not use".

## Test: in a sealed world the probes cannot run, and that is infrastructure

Let v be judgeSubmission(defaultToolchain(), fixtureStatement(), "goal", fixtureProof()).
Expect that v's kind is equal to "infrastructure".

## Test: the four recorded submissions are judged for real

This test may use the real filesystem.
This test may use the real processes.
This test may use the real environment.
Let tc be defaultToolchain().
Let verified be judgeSubmission(tc, fixtureStatement(), "goal", fixtureProof()).
Expect that verified's kind is equal to "verified".
Let wrong be judgeSubmission(tc, fixtureStatement(), "goal", "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.").
Expect that wrong's kind is equal to "rejected".
Expect that wrong's block is equal to 4.
Let admitted be judgeSubmission(tc, fixtureStatement(), "goal", "Axiom zero_add_axiom : forall (n : Nat), Eq Nat (Nat_add Zero n) n.\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by exact zero_add_axiom.").
Expect that admitted's kind is equal to "admitted_axiom".
Expect that admitted's axioms is equal to ["zero_add_axiom"].
Let unparsed be judgeSubmission(tc, fixtureStatement(), "goal", "Theorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; induction n as [ | k ih; rfl.").
Expect that unparsed's kind is equal to "rejected".

## Test: a submission that shadows a prelude name is caught by its probe

This test may use the real filesystem.
This test may use the real processes.
This test may use the real environment.
Let shadow be "Definition Nat_add : Nat -> Nat -> Nat := fun (a : Nat) => fun (b : Nat) => b.\n\nTheorem proof_of_goal : goal := by intro n; rfl.".
Let v be judgeSubmission(defaultToolchain(), fixtureStatement(), "goal", shadow).
Expect that v's kind is equal to "rejected".
Expect that v's error is equal to "the submission declares Nat_add, which the statement or the prelude already binds".

## Test: statements are judged well-formed or not, for real

This test may use the real filesystem.
This test may use the real processes.
This test may use the real environment.
Let tc be defaultToolchain().
Expect that (judgeStatement(tc, fixtureStatement(), "goal"))'s kind is equal to "verified".
Let notProp be judgeStatement(tc, "Definition goal : Nat := Zero.", "goal").
Expect that notProp's kind is equal to "rejected".
Let shadowing be judgeStatement(tc, "Definition False : Prop := True.\n\nDefinition goal : Prop := False.", "goal").
Expect that shadowing's kind is equal to "rejected".
Expect that shadowing's error is equal to "the statement declares False, which the prelude already binds".

## Test: every example in the kernel reference is what the kernel says it is

This test may use the real filesystem.
This test may use the real processes.
This test may use the real environment.
Expect that referenceProblems(defaultToolchain()) is equal to [].
